.TH LSOF 8 "August 27, 2026" "Kandelo" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
lsof \- list open files from /proc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsof
.RB [ \-p
.IR pid ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR command ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B lsof
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsof
lists open file descriptors for processes on the running Kandelo
machine by reading the kernel's
.I /proc
filesystem: it enumerates the numeric entries of
.I /proc
as process IDs, and for each one reads
.I /proc/PID/cmdline
for the command name and walks
.I /proc/PID/fd
to report each open descriptor's target.
.PP
This is an in-tree, minimal implementation
.RI ( examples/lsof.c )
tailored to this kernel's procfs. It is not the upstream lsof and
implements only the behavior documented on this page: it has no
network, user, or PID-namespace filters, no sorting, and no
field-selection or output-formatting options.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B lsof
lists every open file descriptor of every process it can enumerate.
The
.B \-p
and
.B \-c
options and the
.I file
operand may be combined; an entry is printed only when it satisfies
every filter given. A process whose
.I /proc/PID/fd
directory cannot be opened (for example because the process has
already exited, or the directory is not readable) contributes no
output and is not reported as an error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " pid"
List only open file descriptors belonging to the process whose ID is
.IR pid .
.I pid
is parsed as a decimal integer; a value that is not a positive
integer (including non-numeric text) leaves the PID filter disabled.
.TP
.BI \-c " command"
List only processes whose command name contains
.I command
as a substring. The command name is the final path component of the
first NUL-separated field of
.IR /proc/PID/cmdline ,
or
.B ?
if that file cannot be read.
.TP
.I file
List only open file descriptors whose target is exactly
.IR file .
The target is the literal string returned by reading the
.I /proc/PID/fd/N
symbolic link; it is compared verbatim and is not resolved or
canonicalized. If more than one non-option argument is given, only
the last one is used.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage message and exit.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.B lsof
prints one header line, then one line per matching open file
descriptor, in the order
.I /proc
happens to enumerate processes and descriptors (this order is not
guaranteed to be sorted by PID or file descriptor number). Each line
has five whitespace-separated fields:
.TP
.B COMMAND
The process's command name, as described under
.B \-c
above.
.TP
.B PID
The process ID.
.TP
.B FD
The file descriptor number.
.TP
.B TYPE
The kind of file the descriptor refers to, determined by
.BR stat (2)
on the descriptor's target:
.BR REG ", " DIR ", " CHR ", " BLK ", or " FIFO
for regular files, directories, character devices, block devices, and
named pipes; lower-case
.B sock
for sockets. If
.BR stat (2)
fails,
.B lsof
falls back to inspecting the raw target text: a target beginning with
.B pipe:
is reported as
.BR FIFO ,
one beginning with
.B socket:
is reported as
.BR sock ,
and anything else is reported as
.BR unknown .
.TP
.B NAME
The raw target of the descriptor, as returned by
.BR readlink (2)
on
.IR /proc/PID/fd/N .
For an open regular file or directory this is its path; for an
anonymous pipe or socket it is a synthetic string such as
.I pipe:[12345]
supplied by the kernel's procfs, not a filesystem path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B lsof
exits 0 on success, including after
.BR \-h / \-\-help .
It exits 1 if
.I /proc
cannot be opened, or if it is given an option it does not recognize.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc
Process and file-descriptor state enumerated by this tool.
.SH BUGS
.B lsof
does not implement upstream lsof's option set (there is no
.BR \-i ", " \-u ", " \-t ", " \-n ", " +D ,
or similar); it does not sort its output; and it reports neither file
offsets, sizes, locks, nor inode numbers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR proc (5)
